			text="{=connacht_kingdom_text}Kingdom of Connacht formed the political core of the alliance, driven by the ambitions of the Ua Conchobair dynasty to expand influence across western Ireland. Kingdom of Meath joined the coalition largely out of necessity, as the Ua Mael Sechlainn rulers sought protection against growing pressure from both northern and southern rivals.  Kingdom of Breifne entered the alliance as a flexible frontier power, with the Ua Ruairc and Ua Ragallaig clans balancing loyalty and opportunism between stronger neighbors. Together, these kingdoms were loosely united by shifting marriage ties, hostage exchanges, and shared interest in resisting over-dominance by Munster and Leinster. Although never a formal state, the Connacht–Meath–Breifne alignment was a practical political network that shaped much of Ireland’s power struggles around the year 1100.">
